{{also|scherzò|Scherzo}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|scherzo||joke, play}}, from {{m|it|scherzare||to joke, jest}}, from [[w:Italian language|Old ]]{{cog|it|scherzare}}, from {{etyl|lng|en}} {{m|goh|*skerzan||to jump merrily, enjoy oneself, jest}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*skirtaną||to hop, jump}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*(s)kerǝd-||to dance, jump}}. Akin to {{cog|gmh|scherzen||to frolic, jump merrily, hop up and down}} ({{cog|de|scherzen||to joke}}, {{m|de|Scherz||joke, sport}}), {{cog|no|skjerta||to joke}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈskɛətsəʊ/|/ˈskɜːt-/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{enPR|skĕrʹtsō(')|skĕrtʹsō(')}}, {{IPA|/ˈskɛəɹ(ˌ)tsoʊ/|/ˈskɛəɹtˌsoʊ/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɛəɹtsəʊ|ɜː(ɹ)tsəʊ|ɛə(ɹ)tsəʊ|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|scher|zo|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|scherzos|scherzi}}

# {{lb|en|music}} A piece of music or a [[movement]] from a larger piece such as a [[symphony]]; especially, a piece of music played in a playful manner.
#* '''1980''', Anthony Burgess, ''Earthly Powers'':
#*: At seven Val knocked – three shorts and one long, out of the '''scherzo''' of Beethoven’s Fifth – and I rushed to open.

